<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scottish Enlightenment</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Scottish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> was the flourishing of philosophy, science and scholarship in eighteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, centred on the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>. Its figures included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hume">David Hume</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-ferguson">Adam Ferguson</a>, Thomas Reid and the geologist James Hutton. It made major contributions to modern philosophy, economics, history and the social sciences.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scottish-enlightenment.jpg" alt="Scottish Enlightenment" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kim Traynor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Hume_and_Adam_Smith_statues,_Edinburgh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
